WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.836
[MUSIC]

00:00:03.836 --> 00:00:07.265
I want you to stop for a second and think
about, when I say customer experience for

00:00:07.265 --> 00:00:08.900
you, what does that mean?

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.630
If you are anything like the students that
I normally see in class, what you think

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:16.170
about your customer experience is
literally the time in which the customers

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:21.300
interact with whatever service person
you have in your organization.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:23.900
That's the kind of answer that I
typically get when people think about

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:24.770
customer experience.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:28.830
But then what I do is I ask people
talk to me about your last vacation.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.290
And I want you to spend a minute and
think about your last vacation.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:33.350
Describe to yourself or

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.450
sort of recreate in your mind
what that experience was like.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:40.980
But when I ask them about their
experience as going on vacation,

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.960
they talk about planning the vacation,
anticipating the vacation, what they

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.840
do when they go on vacation and then
the memories that they have from vacation.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:55.710
The customer experience is not simply the
very narrow interaction with your product.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.925
It also includes a before and after.

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:03.720
And those befores an afters are critical
to help people understand your product and

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.360
for your likelihood of achieving
the holy grail which is word of mouth.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.380
So in psychology,
we talk about three sources of utility.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.960
Utility is kind of like a measure
of psychological well being,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.100
more utility is something better.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.390
Anticipated utility,
how much utility will I get?

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.540
Okay, I think about buying a car,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.000
my experience with the car is
not just driving the car, but

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:27.690
it's also thinking about which car to buy,
getting excited about that car.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.670
Experienced utility, how much utility or

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:36.230
how much well being am I experiencing from
the actual use of the product itself?

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:40.530
Okay, and this is typically when people
think about a customer experience,

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:42.900
they really focus on
the experience utility component.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.210
Retrospective utility.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.470
What do I remember this to be like?

00:01:47.470 --> 00:01:50.640
And the memory is extremely important
because the memory is what's going to

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.160
determine your word of mouth.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:56.070
All three of those need to be managed to
create a really truly winning customer

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:56.840
experience.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.870
Experiences aren't flat, right?

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:01.770
You don't sort of experience
the same thing at all times.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:02.990
You have a moment of delight.

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.110
You have a moment of despair.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:05.810
You have a moment of meh.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.680
The way memory works is
in terms of snapshots,

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.120
I remember moments in that experience.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.370
And why is that so critical because it
tells you that what you need to manage is

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:20.660
not the film but
it's specific kind of moments.

